<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Thaba Nchu</h1><p class="st-meta">1833 CE – 1884 CE · capital Thaba Nchu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Barolong chiefdom of Moroka at Thaba Nchu, an ally of the Voortrekkers that survived as an enclave until 1884.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1833 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a> boo Seleka under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroka-ii">Moroka II</a>, together with Wesleyan missionaries, moved from Platberg on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaal-river">Vaal</a> to the mountain of Thaba Nchu, on land obtained from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a>. Moroka's claim to have bought it was later disputed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Basotho Kingdom</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a> were Tswana-speaking farmers and herders with a strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodist</a> mission. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroka-ii">Moroka</a> gave food, oxen and refuge to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekker</a> parties after their defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vegkop">Vegkop</a> in 1836, and Thaba Nchu stayed allied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>. It remained a self-governing enclave inside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State</a>, whose government recognised its independence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroka-ii">Moroka</a>'s death in 1880 his sons fought over the succession. The Free State used the turmoil to annex Thaba Nchu in 1884, though part of the land was later incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bophuthatswana">Bophuthatswana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/thaba-nchu.jpg" alt="Thaba Nchu" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Rana Thakar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graham_Maclachlan_-_Thaba_N%27chu_Mountain_in_the_distance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1833 CE – 1884 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thaba Nchu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moroka II, Tshipinare</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Tswana traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Setswana</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>South Africa</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Barolong of Thaba Nchu, Moroka's state</dd></dl></aside></div>
